NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows the 1-Wire interpreter displaying a search sequence, device address 
and data read from from a Maxim DS1920 temperature sensor.

A cursor has been place next to each of several points of interest in this captured data. 
From within the Waveforms or State List tabs, you can quickly scroll to cursors A through F 
by pressing the <A> through <F> keys on your keyboard. 

By zooming in near each of the cursors, the following sequence can be observed:

     You can quickly zoom in or out by pressing the <+> or <-> keys on the numerical 
     keypad of your keyboard. 

At cursor A, a "Master Reset Pulse" occurs, followed by a "Slave Presence Pulse". The 
LogicPort has been setup to trigger on this sequence.

     You can press <T> on your keyboard to quickly scroll to the trigger position.

At cursor B, the master controller sends the "Search ROM" command F0h, followed by the 
search sequence used to identify devices on the network.

At cursor C, the master controller sends a "Match ROM" command 55h, followed by the 
unique 64 bit address of the DS1920.

At cursor D, the master controller sends the "Read Scratchpad" command BEh and 
immediately reads 9 bytes of memory from the DS1920. The first two bytes of that memory 
contain (LSB first) the temperature measured by the DS1920. The resolution is 0.5 degrees 
celsius, so the value of 34h (52 decimal) indicates a temperatue of 26 degrees.

At cursor E, the master begins another search sequence to see if any new devices have 
been connected.

     You can right click on the "Signal" cell of the waveform table and select 
     "Expand All" from the drop down menu to quickly expand all of the interpreters.
     This exposes the individual signals allowing you to observe the original raw data.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain intact
for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning purposes, 
but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the "Save As" option to 
save an editable copy of any example file.
/
